Tracing the actual flow: unstakeAndExitPool runs as a delegatecall from the vault into CurveConvexLib, so in _exitPool
  the receiver argument passed to ICurve2TokenPoolV2.remove_liquidity{,_one_coin} is literally address(this) — the
  vault/lib itself, never an address the attacker chooses. When the Curve V2 pool performs its raw_call to push native
  ETH with use_eth = true, the only code that executes as a result is the receiver's receive() — here an empty receive()
  external payable {} body (or the vault's equivalent) that performs no external calls and hands control straight back
  to the pool.

  For a reentrancy window to exist, some attacker-controlled contract must get execution inside it. In this path there
  is none: the callee list is fixed and consists only of the immutable Curve pool, the immutable gauge/Convex reward
  pool, WETH, and the vault's own empty ETH receiver. The pool address, gauge, Convex reward pool, pool token and
  curveInterface are all constructor-set immutables (deployment configuration), and TOKEN_1/TOKEN_2 are read from
  ICurvePool.coins() at construction — an unprivileged caller cannot substitute any of them. Nothing in _exitPool,
  _unstakeLpTokens, or the WETH deposit{value:} wrap in unstakeAndExitPool transfers control to caller-supplied code;
  poolClaim, _minAmounts and isSingleSided are pure value parameters that never become call targets.

  The claim also inverts what _checkReentrancyContext() is for: it is a probe the money market invokes so that a read
  attempted while Curve's internal @nonreentrant('lock') is held reverts. During the vault's own remove_liquidity,
  Curve's lock is in fact held, so any attempt to price through a path that calls _checkReentrancyContext() reverts
  rather than reading a stale/manipulated state — and in any case no attacker frame exists in that window to attempt it.
  An attacker cannot interleave a separate transaction into the middle of the vault's exit call; ETH landing in a
  contract's receive() does not give a third party execution.

  No step was identified where an unprivileged external actor gains control flow while t_CurrentAccount is set. The
  claimed exploit does not hold.

  CONSTRAINT|C21|A reentrancy claim must identify a concrete point inside the alleged window where attacker-controlled
  code actually receives execution; a value transfer whose recipient is the protocol's own contract with an inert
  fallback hands control to the protocol, not to any external party, and configuration-fixed callees do not supply that
  entry point.
  VERDICT|REFUTED|src/single-sided-lp/CurveConvex2Token.sol:_exitPool:263|C21|The receiver passed to the Curve V2
  removal is hardcoded to address(this), so the ETH push only invokes the protocol's own empty receive(); every other
  callee in the path is a constructor-set immutable, leaving no attacker-controlled code executing inside the supposed
  reentrancy window.
